    "<!-- __Proof.__ See the notes. -->\n",
    "\n",
    "<br>\n",
    "\n",
    "__Example.__ Let $A = \\begin{bmatrix}3&6&-8\\\\0&0&6\\\\0&0&2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $B = \\begin{bmatrix}4&0&0\\\\-2&1&0\\\\5&3&4\\end{bmatrix}$. \n",
    "\n",
    "Then the eigenvalues of $A$ are 3, 0, and 2.   The eigenvalues of $B$ are 4 and 1.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## Multiplicity\n",
    "\n",
    ":::{style=\"font-size: .8em\"}\n",
    "Note that one of the eigenvalues of $B$ occurs twice. We say that it is _repeated_. \n",
    "\n",
    "<br>\n",
    "\n",
    "In general, every eigenvalue has a certain __algebraic multiplicity__. Algebraic multiplicity counts the repetitions of $\\lambda$ among the eigenvalues.\n",
    "\n",
    "<br>\n",
    "\n",
    "The eigenvalue 4 of $B$ has algebraic mulitiplicity of 2, while the eigenvalue 1 has algebraic multiplicity of 1. \n",
    "\n",
    "<br>\n",
    "\n",
    "Next to algebraic multiplicity, there is also the __geometric multiplicity__ of an eigenvalue. The geometric multiplicty counts the linearly independent eigenvectors for $\\lambda.$\n",
    "\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## Multiplicity\n",
    "\n",
    ":::{style=\"font-size: .8em\"}\n",
    "\n",
    "Consider $C = \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}.$ \n",
    "\n",
    "* $C$ has one (repeated) eigenvlaue: 0.\n",
    "* There is only one eigenvector describing the eigenspace of $\\lambda =0$: $\\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\end{bmatrix}.$\n",
    "* The algebraic multiplicity of $\\lambda = 0$ is 2.\n",
    "* The geometric multiplicity of $\\lambda = 0$ is 1.\n"
