    "## Warnings\n",
    "\n",
    ":::{style=\"font-size: .8em\"}\n",
    "2. Elimination does not preserve eigenvalues.\n",
    "\n",
    "If you add a row of a matrix to its other row, or exchange its rows, the eigenvalues usually change.\n",
    "\n",
    "__Example.__  Consider $A = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 6 \\end{bmatrix}$.\n",
    "\n",
    "* Its characteristic equation is $(1-\\lambda)(6-\\lambda) - 6 = \\lambda^2 - 7 \\lambda = \\lambda (\\lambda - 7) = 0.$\n",
    "Hence, its eigenvalues are 0 and 7.\n",
    "* The reduced echelon form of $A$ is $U = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}.$ The eigenvalues of $U$ are 0 and 1.\n",
    "\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## Back to Markov Chains\n",
    "\n",
    ":::{style=\"font-size: .8em\"}\n",
    "Recall that a _Markov chain_ models systems that change over time. It imposes a fixed rule that tells us how to compute the next state of the system based on the current state of the system (no matter the prior history or which time period we're currently at).\n",
    "\n",
    "Formally, a Markov chain is represented by:\n",
    "\n",
    "- An initial probability vector $\\mathbf{x_0} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ showing the fraction of people/objects in each state at the beginning of the system, and\n",
    "- A stochastic matrix $P \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n \\times n}$ that encodes the state transition rule, in the sense that the next state is calculated as ${\\bf x_{k+1}} = P{\\bf x_k}$.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## Back to Markov Chains\n",
    "\n",
    ":::{style=\"font-size: .8em\"}\n",
    "In the long run, it can be shown that every stochastic matrix has at least one __steady-state vector__ ${\\bf v}$ such that $P{\\bf v} = {\\bf v}$. That is, ${\\bf v}$ is an eigenvector of $P.$\n",
    "\n",
    "To find the steady-state vector for a Markov Chain:\n",
    "\n",
    "* Solve the linear system $(P-I){\\bf x} = {\\bf 0}.$  \n",
    "* The system will have an infinite number of solutions, with one free variable.  Obtain a general solution.\n",
    "* Pick any specific solution (choose any value for the free variable), and scale it so the entries add up to 1."
